  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nQuiet and dry weather remains the story through Tuesday as the \nsurface high gradually shifts east and return flow develops. \nMostly sunny skies and southwest winds will help temperatures \nclimb into the 70s Tuesday, with some areas potentially reaching\nthe upper 70s. Despite the warmer afternoons, overnight \ntemperatures will continue to fall back into the 40s. Patchy/ \nareas of fog look likely late tonight into Tuesday morning, \nparticularly across interior locations. \n\nThe pattern begins to change Tuesday night into Wednesday as a \nnorthern-stream wave moves into the Great Lakes and pulls deeper\nmoisture northward. Shower chances will increase late Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday spreading west to east, with the better \nchances coming late Wednesday into Thursday as the wave and \nassociated boundary track through. The system is currently \nfavored to pass through primarily during the nighttime hours, \nwhile relatively dry low and mid-levels should limit how \nefficiently precipitation reaches the ground, especially farther\neast. Northwest Lower and eastern Upper appear to have the \nbetter opportunity for measurable rainfall, with guidance \nsuggesting medium chances (50 to 70 percent) for at least 0.50\",\nand low to medium chances (30 to 60 percent) for locally higher\namounts around 1\" possible mainly along the shoreline of Lake \nMichigan. Lesser amounts are expected the farther east one goes.\n\nThe midweek wave then gradually departs Thursday night into \nFriday, allowing the pattern to turn quieter once again. \nTemperatures will trend cooler behind the system, with highs \nsettling back into the 60s and overnight lows generally in the \n40s or cooler through the weekend. Some lingering low-end shower\nchances could continue into Friday, but with high pressure \nbuilding back in, ample dry time is expected and looks to \ncontinue as we head into the weekend.",
